==English==

===Etymology===
A twentieth-century borrowing from {{bor|en|grc|ἐμπάθεια|lit=passion|notext=1}} (formed from {{m|grc|ἐν||in, at}} + {{m|grc|πάθος||feeling}}), coined by {{w|Edward B. Titchener|Edward Bradford Titchener}} to translate {{cog|de|Einfühlung}}. The modern word in {{cog|el|εμπάθεια}} has an opposite meaning denoting strong negative feelings and prejudice against someone.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɛmpəθi/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{wikipedia}}
{{en-noun|~|empathies}}

# Identification with or understanding of the thoughts, feelings, or emotional state of another person.
#: ''She had a lot of '''empathy''' for her neighbor; she knew what it was like to lose a parent too.''
# {{lb|en|parapsychology|science fiction}} A [[paranormal]] ability to [[psychically]] read another person's emotions.

====Usage notes====
Used similarly to {{m|en|sympathy}}, interchangeably in looser usage. In stricter usage, '''empathy''' is stronger and more intimate, meaning that the subject understands and shares an emotion with the object—as in “I feel your pain”—while {{m|en||sympathy}} is weaker and more distant—concern, but not shared emotion: “I care for you”.

